    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you expand a bit on the risks of vertical scaling? specifically regarding what happens if the hardware itself undergoes a sudden failure or if the upgrade process causes downtime. i want to make sure the stakeholder understands the 'single point of failure' part.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling introduces significant operational risk because the entire application relies on a single physical machine, making any component failure an immediate outage for all users. If that one hardware unit or its internal parts (like a CPU or disk) fail, there is no redundant backup system to take over traffic, leading to a complete and often prolonged service disruption. Furthermore, the upgrade process often requires shutting down the server to install new, more powerful components, which forces the whole application into an idle state until the replacement is finished. This creates an unavoidable window of downtime that horizontal scaling can largely eliminate through automatic failover to other healthy nodes.",
      "source": "target",
